<h2>India's largest chilli market by a wide margin</h2>
<p>Most buyers don't associate Indian spice exports with China first, but the trade data is unambiguous: China takes more Indian dried red chilli than any other single country, and by a wide margin — roughly USD 456 million in 2024. This isn't a niche or emerging relationship; it's the anchor market for the whole Indian chilli export trade, built on food-processing and oleoresin-extraction demand operating at genuinely industrial scale.</p>

<h2>Processing-scale demand changes what "quality" means</h2>
<p>A buyer running an oleoresin-extraction line or a continuous food-processing operation cares about something slightly different from a buyer sourcing chilli for direct retail use: lot-to-lot consistency in colour value and pungency. Variation that a smaller culinary buyer might not notice shows up immediately in a large processing run. If you're buying at this scale, specify your ASTA colour and Scoville tolerance explicitly, and expect us to confirm both on the certificate of analysis for your specific lot rather than presenting a single fixed number across all chilli.</p>

<h2>GACC registration is the real gate, not the shipping lane</h2>
<p>China's Decree 248 requires facility-level registration with GACC before many food categories, dried chilli included, can clear Chinese customs — registration of the producing/processing facility itself, separate from any trading relationship. This is the item that actually determines whether a first China order moves smoothly: confirm registration status at the enquiry stage, because it's a process with real lead time that cannot be resolved once a shipment is already booked.</p>

<h2>China's own standards, not a borrowed EU or US clearance</h2>
<p>China sets its residue and quality standards independently of the EU or US systems. Chilli tested and documented for a European buyer is not automatically compliant for a Chinese one. Tell us early that China is your destination and we test and certify specifically against the applicable Chinese requirement.</p>

